NU-1000 Adsorption Studies per Gram. The amount of selenate or selenite adsorbed per gram of NU-1000 was determined by exposing \(5\;\mathrm{mg}\) of NU-1000 to \(10~\mathrm{mL}\) of an aqueous solution of selenium as sodium selenite or sodium selenate in a \(15~\mathrm{mL}\) polypropylene centrifuge tube with concentrations of ca. 18, 27, 36, 45 and \(55~\mathrm{ppm}\) . These concentrations correspond to an exposure level of 1.00, 1.50, 2.00, 2.50 and 3.00 analyte molecules per \(Z\mathbf{r}_{\mathrm{r}}\) -node of NU-1000. The solutions were centrifuged for 30 seconds to allow the MOF to settle to the bottom of the tube. Aliquots of the supernatant were removed and diluted to \(10~\mathrm{mL}\) in \(3\ \%\) \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}\) at 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 10, 15, 30, 60, 90, 120 and 180 minutes for analysis by ICP-OES. ICP-OES was used to determine the concentration of Se, \(Z\mathbf{r}\) and Na in each solution. Comparison of control solutions to those containing MOF was used to determine the amount of selenate or selenite adsorbed (q) in \(\mathrm{mg/g}\) of NU-1000 where q \(=(\mathrm{Ci}-\mathrm{Cf})\mathrm{~x~V/m}\) , \(\mathrm{Ci}=\) initial concentration, \(\mathrm{Cf}=\) final concentration, \(\mathrm{V}=\) volume of solution exposed to NU-1000 and m \(=\) mass of NU-1000 in g.
